// An ephemeral message bus, as the vocabulary's `bus` kind.
//
// # It is not a stream, and the difference is what is kept
//
// A stream is a pubsub topic that reaches every subscriber. A bus is n:n and
// EPHEMERAL — "everything attached gets what is sent, and nothing is kept" —
// which is what makes it the thing a browser attaches to over a websocket and
// not the thing a job is queued on.
//
// # Only publish, and its other verb is a different mechanism from stream's
//
// `subscribe` on a bus is an ATTACHMENT: a websocket at the bus's own host, or
// a workload the platform delivers to. Fan-out is not a question here the way
// it is for a stream — everything attached gets what is sent is the kind's own
// definition — but the attaching is the gateway's to serve and not a function a
// guest calls.
package taubyte:bus@0.1.0;

interface publisher {
    variant error {
        no-such-bus(string),
        denied(string),
        unavailable(string),
    }

    // publish sends one message to everything attached. It returns when the
    // message has been handed to the swarm and promises nothing about who was
    // attached — nothing is kept, so an unattached bus drops it.
    publish: func(name: string, message: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;
}

world imports {
    import publisher;
}
